// Benchmark regression gate (#2931): builds and runs bench/cases/{collatz,
// mandelbrot,fib,alloc,churn,json,map,matrix,sort,strings} with the compiler
// under test and fails when peak RSS or user CPU time regresses past 1.5x a
// baseline recorded below, OR when allocated object count leaves a TWO-SIDED
// band (#3130, see "WHY OBJECT COUNT IS TWO-SIDED" below). "churn" (#2978) is
// a leak guard, not a speed benchmark: a live set of one object allocated
// 4,000,000 times, run with BIT_GC_MIN_KB pinned via Baseline.gcMinKb -- see
// runtime/gc/gcindex.bit's idxGrow (#2756/#2922) for the bug this catches.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. docs/perf-regression-rca.md records a 4-5x compute
// regression and an 18x peak-RSS regression that shipped across many
// releases with every gate in `./make test` staying green, because nothing
// in the suite measured performance at all — both were only found by a human
// running `bench/run.sh` by hand, 1470 commits after the fact (#2920,
// #2921). This is the gate that would have caught them the day they landed.
//
// WHY NOT `bench/run.sh`. That script needs `go` and `cc` on PATH, runs 7
// iterations per case per LANGUAGE (Bit, Go, C), and rewrites three tracked
// files (README.md, bench/RESULTS.md, bench/history.csv) — a gate must not
// mutate the tree, and 7x3 external-toolchain builds is not a suite cost.
// This measures Bit only, against baselines recorded in this file rather
// than read from bench/history.csv: its newest rows predate #2920 and still
// encode the bug (`alloc` RSS = 161529856), which is exactly the number
// #2920 fixed.
//
// WHY PEAK RSS AND USER CPU, NEVER WALL CLOCK. This repo already has three
// open tickets (#2401, #2415, #2422) about absolute wall-clock deadlines
// reddening the suite purely from host load. Peak RSS is a kernel-tracked
// high-water mark, unaffected by what else the box is doing. User CPU time is
// time actually spent executing on a core, not time spent waiting for one —
// measured on this host while writing this gate, `user` stayed within 0.06s
// across 7 runs of the same binary. `real` (wall clock) is never read below.
//
// WHY MINIMUM OF N, NOT MEAN. Contention and scheduling noise can only ever
// make one run cost MORE than its true floor, never less, so the minimum of
// `reps` independent runs is the statistic noise cannot inflate — the same
// reasoning tests/bit/schedbench.bit's header gives for context-switch
// timing.
//
// WHY 1.5x, AND WHY IT NEVER FIRES ON AN IMPROVEMENT. The regressions this
// gate exists to catch were 4-5x and 18x — the bar only needs to clear gross
// breakage, not police day-to-day noise. The check is a strict
// greater-than against `baseline * 1.5`, so a benchmark that gets faster or
// leaner can only pass more easily, never fail. CPU (not RSS) can override
// this per case via Baseline.cpuCeilingPct — fib does (#2982), because its
// baseline is small enough in absolute terms that 1.5x cannot absorb real
// multi-agent scheduling noise; see fib's own baseline-table comment.
//
// WHY OBJECT COUNT IS TWO-SIDED (#3130), UNLIKE RSS/CPU ABOVE. The 1.5x bar
// above is deliberately one-directional because RSS and CPU are noisy: a
// benchmark that gets faster or leaner can only pass more easily, and that
// is correct, but it also means the gate protects yesterday's slow number
// forever — a later regression back to it still passes. Allocated object
// count (`swept+live` from `BIT_GC_STATS=1`, see measureScript's header
// below) has no such noise: it is EXACTLY deterministic, the same binary
// reporting the identical count on every run — verified 5x on every one of
// this file's 10 macOS/arm64 cases (#3130's ticket comment has all 15+
// readings). Determinism is what makes a TWO-SIDED band sound: failing past
// 1.05x (objCeiling) catches a regression the same way 1.5x does above, and
// failing below 0.5x (objFloor) catches an IMPROVEMENT that landed with no
// baseline update, which is the ratchet's whole point — a benchmark that
// gets leaner must have Baseline.objects re-recorded, not silently protect
// the old, higher number. Gate on objects, never on `collections`: the
// collection count depends on WHEN the trigger fires, which depends on live
// bytes, which #2941 currently inflates 8x — gating on it would produce
// exactly the flaky red that gets gates disabled. An absent or malformed
// `BIT_GC_STATS` line is a hard FAIL, never a pass (runCase's `m.objects < 0`
// check) — the line prints once, on the child's OWN stderr, only at a clean
// exit, so killing the process or discarding its stderr both produce
// nothing, and nothing must never parse as zero.
//
// WHY OUTPUTS ARE ASSERTED TOO. A benchmark that returns the wrong answer
// quickly is exactly the failure mode a pure-timing gate cannot see, so
// every run's stdout is compared byte-for-byte against the known-correct
// value before its cost is even considered.
//
// SUBPROCESS MECHANICS, as tests/bit/golden.bit and tests/bit/lspcache.bit:
// each case is built once with the compiler under test, then run `reps`
// times through a generated `/bin/sh` wrapper via `osRunBounded` (no rusage
// primitive is reachable from Bit itself). The wrapper is the only
// platform-specific part: macOS's `/usr/bin/time -l` reports both user CPU
// seconds and maximum resident set size (bytes) from ONE run; Linux has no
// such tool guaranteed present, so it backgrounds the binary and polls
// `/proc/<pid>/status` (`VmHWM`, itself a kernel-maintained high-water mark —
// any read taken late in the run already reflects the true peak) and
// `/proc/<pid>/stat` (utime+stime, the same two fields
// tests/bit/fsblock.bit's `cpu()` helper already reads and was mutation-
// tested for, #2207).
//
// Run from the repository root:
//   ./bit-out/bin/bit run tests/bit/benchgate
// Needs BIT_BIN (the compiler under test), BIT_LIBBITRT (the runtime
// archive to link against) and BIT_BENCH_DIR (bench/cases) — no defaults.
//
// #3023 split this file into `tests/bit/benchgate/{benchgate,baselines}.bit`:
// the per-platform baseline tables (`Baseline`, `platformName`,
// `baselinesMacosArm64`, `baselinesLinuxX64`, `baselinesLinuxArm64`,
// `baselinesFor`) now live in the sibling `baselines.bit`, no `import`, one
// shared scope. This file dropped from 809 to 534 lines and kept the ceiling
// math, the shell-wrapper plumbing, the per-case runner and `main`.

import { env, envOr, exit } from "std/os"
import { readFile, writeFile, exists, mkdir } from "std/fs"
import { trimSpace, parseInt, split } from "std/strings"

// Independent timed runs per case; the MINIMUM of each series is compared to
// baseline. 3 costs roughly one pass of the 4 cases' combined baseline CPU
// (~4.6s) times 3, plus 4 one-time builds — under 20s measured, inside the
// ~30s budget #2931 set (#2747's lint gate, at 21s, is the precedent for
// what a suite addition may cost).
const reps: int = 3

// FAIL when measured > baseline * 3 / 2 (i.e. > 1.5x). Integer arithmetic
// throughout so there is no float-rounding edge case in the comparison. Used
// for RSS on every case, and for CPU on every case except where
// Baseline.cpuCeilingPct overrides it -- see cpuCeiling() below and #2982.
fn ceiling(baseline: int): int {
  return baseline * 3 / 2
}

// CPU ceiling, per-case (#2982). `ceiling()` above is 150 (1.5x) for every
// case; fib overrides Baseline.cpuCeilingPct to 190 on macOS/arm64 ONLY,
// because its baseline there (29cs) is too small in absolute terms for 1.5x
// to absorb real scheduling noise in this gate's actual operating
// environment (a single unshared run, per the full-suite-owns-this-gate
// note below). fib on Linux x64 keeps the blanket 150 deliberately — its
// baseline there (101cs) gives 1.5x enough absolute headroom that the same
// defect does not apply; see fib's own baseline-table comment in each
// platform's table for the measurements. Integer arithmetic, same reasoning
// as ceiling() above.
fn cpuCeiling(b: Baseline): int {
  return b.cpuCentis * b.cpuCeilingPct / 100
}

// Object-count band (#3130), TWO-SIDED unlike ceiling()/cpuCeiling() above.
// Allocated object count (swept+live read from one BIT_GC_STATS=1 run, see
// gcObjects() below) is EXACTLY deterministic — the same binary reports the
// identical count on every run, verified 5x on every one of the 10
// macOS/arm64 cases (pasted in #3130's ticket comment) — so this guard can
// fail in BOTH directions. Past 1.05x (objCeiling) catches a regression the
// same way ceiling() does; below 0.5x (objFloor) catches an IMPROVEMENT that
// was never recorded as a new baseline, which is the reason #3130 exists: a
// benchmark that gets leaner must have Baseline.objects updated, not
// silently protect the old, slower number forever. Baseline.objects == -1
// means "not measured on this platform" (see its field comment in
// baselines.bit) — the band check is skipped for that case, but the GC
// stats line itself is still required unconditionally; see runCase's use of
// gcObjects() below for why a missing or malformed line is always a hard
// fail regardless of whether a numeric baseline is on file.
const objCeilPct: int = 105
const objFloorPct: int = 50

fn objCeiling(baseline: int): int {
  return baseline * objCeilPct / 100
}

fn objFloor(baseline: int): int {
  return baseline * objFloorPct / 100
}

// Generous per-subprocess bound; every case finishes in under 3s and this
// only matters if something hangs. `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S` widens it the same
// way every other harness in this suite honors it.
fn timeoutMs(): int {
  let s = parseInt(envOr("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S", "60")) catch _ {
    60
  }
  if (s <= 0) {
    return 60000
  }
  return s * 1000
}

// --- shell plumbing ----------------------------------------------------

// Wraps `s` as exactly one POSIX `/bin/sh` word.
fn shQuote(s: string): string {
  let out = "'"
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(s)) {
    if (s[i] == 39) { // '
      out = out + "'\\''"
    } else {
      out = out + s[i:i + 1]
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out + "'"
}

fn mkdirQuiet(path: string) {
  mkdir(path) catch e {
    return
  }
}

// A scratch root whose path is already lexically canonical (the trailing-
// slash hazard is tests/bit/lspcache.bit's #2410 — `TMPDIR` ends in `/` on
// macOS, and a doubled separator has bitten a gate here before).
fn scratchRoot(): string {
  let base = envOr("TMPDIR", "/tmp")
  while (len(base) > 1 && base[len(base) - 1] == '/') {
    base = base[0:len(base) - 1]
  }
  return base + "/bit-benchgate-${timeUnixNs()}"
}

// `osRunBounded`'s three non-zero outcomes rendered as a message (ABI.md
// §19), same encoding as tests/bit/stress/verify.bit's `runMsg`.
fn runMsg(code: int): string {
  if (code == 0) {
    return ""
  }
  if (code == 0 - 1) {
    return "SPAWN FAILED (fork/exec/wait error)"
  }
  if (code == 0 - 2) {
    return "TIMED OUT after ${timeoutMs()}ms (raise BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S)"
  }
  if (code <= 0 - 100) {
    return "CRASHED: killed by signal ${0 - code - 100}"
  }
  return "exited ${code}"
}

// --- build ---------------------------------------------------------------

fn buildScript(
  bit: string, libbitrt: string, stdlib: string, srcPath: string,
  binPath: string, outPath: string, errPath: string,
): string {
  let s = "#!/bin/sh\n"
  if (stdlib != "") {
    s = s + "BIT_STDLIB=" + shQuote(stdlib) + "\nexport BIT_STDLIB\n"
  }
  if (libbitrt != "") {
    s = s + "BIT_LIBBITRT=" + shQuote(libbitrt) + "\nexport BIT_LIBBITRT\n"
  }
  s = s +
    "exec " +
    shQuote(bit) +
    " build " +
    shQuote(srcPath) +
    " -o " +
    shQuote(binPath) +
    " > " +
    shQuote(outPath) +
    " 2> " +
    shQuote(errPath) +
    "\n"
  return s
}

// --- measure ---------------------------------------------------------------

// One run's user-CPU-and-peak-RSS wrapper. Dispatches on `uname -s` so a
// single generated script serves both gate platforms (macOS dev host, real
// aarch64/x86_64 Linux via scripts/{arm64,x64}gate.sh) — see the file header
// for why each branch works the way it does. The wrapper's own `exit`
// re-raises the BENCHMARK's exit code, so `osRunBounded`'s return value is
// the thing that failed, not "the wrapper script", and non-zero there is
// treated as a hard failure by the caller.
//
// Result file format: one line, FOUR space-separated decimals —
// "<benchmark exit code> <user CPU centiseconds> <peak RSS bytes> <allocated
// objects>" — with -1 standing in for "not measured" on any of the three
// metrics, never a silent 0 (the same convention tests/bit/lspcache.bit's
// `intAfter` uses). The 4th field is `swept+live` parsed out of the child's
// own `BIT_GC_STATS=1` exit-summary line (#3130) — see runtime/root's
// `statsReport` for the line's exact grammar and
// tests/bit/lspcache.bit's header for why `swept+live` is "every object the
// run allocated" and `collections` is not what this gate reads (it depends
// on when the trigger fires, which depends on live bytes — see #2941).
fn measureScript(bin: string, outPath: string, resPath: string, gcMinKb: int): string {
  let rawPath = resPath + ".raw"
  let gcPath = resPath + ".gcerr"
  // `swept=`/`live=` extraction shared (as an awk fragment) by both platform
  // branches below: scan every whitespace-separated field of a captured
  // stream for the two `key=value` tokens (unambiguous — nothing else either
  // platform captures produces a field starting with either prefix) and hold
  // their sum in `obj`, or -1 if the line never arrived. `sub()`'s
  // replacement is `""` so this needs no `[`/`]` escaping — no anchor on
  // `[bit-gc]` itself is needed either.
  let gcAwkBody = "sw=-1;lv=-1} /swept=/{for(i=1;i<=NF;i++){" +
    "if($i~/^swept=/){t=$i;sub(/^swept=/,\"\",t);sw=t} " +
    "if($i~/^live=/){t=$i;sub(/^live=/,\"\",t);lv=t}}} "
  let s = "#!/bin/sh\n"
  // Exported before the platform dispatch below so it reaches the benchmark
  // child on either branch. 0 means "leave BIT_GC_MIN_KB unset" (see the
  // Baseline.gcMinKb field comment). BIT_GC_STATS=1 is unconditional — every
  // case needs an object-count reading now, not just churn's leak guard.
  if (gcMinKb > 0) {
    s = s + "BIT_GC_MIN_KB=${gcMinKb}\nexport BIT_GC_MIN_KB\n"
  }
  s = s + "BIT_GC_STATS=1\nexport BIT_GC_STATS\n"
  s = s + "case \"$(uname -s)\" in\n"
  s = s + "  Darwin)\n"
  // The child inherits fd 2 from this redirect, same as before #3130 — its
  // own `[bit-gc] ...` line and `/usr/bin/time -l`'s summary both land in
  // rawPath, in that order (the child exits and is reaped before `time`
  // appends its own report), so no second capture file is needed here.
  s = s +
    "    /usr/bin/time -l " +
    shQuote(bin) +
    " >" +
    shQuote(outPath) +
    " 2>" +
    shQuote(rawPath) +
    "\n"
  s = s + "    ec=$?\n"
  // `/usr/bin/time -l`'s first line is fixed-format: "<real> real <user> user
  // <sys> sys", so the user-seconds value is always field 3 of the line
  // matching " user". `(u*100)+0.5` rounds to the nearest centisecond. The
  // same pass folds in the `swept=`/`live=` scan above (rawPath already
  // holds the child's own stderr ahead of `time`'s own report — see the
  // comment on the redirect above) so ONE awk call produces all four fields.
  s = s +
    "    awk -v ec=$ec 'BEGIN{u=-1;r=-1;" +
    gcAwkBody +
    "/ user/{u=$3} /maximum resident set size/{r=$1} " +
    "END{obj=-1; if (sw>=0 && lv>=0) obj=sw+lv; " +
    "printf \"%d %d %d %d\\n\", ec, (u*100)+0.5, r, obj}' " +
    shQuote(rawPath) +
    " > " +
    shQuote(resPath) +
    "\n"
  s = s + "    exit $ec\n"
  s = s + "    ;;\n"
  s = s + "  *)\n"
  s = s + "    " + shQuote(bin) + " >" + shQuote(outPath) + " 2>" + shQuote(gcPath) + " &\n"
  s = s + "    pid=$!\n"
  s = s + "    cpu=-1\n"
  s = s + "    hwm=-1\n"
  s = s + "    while kill -0 $pid 2>/dev/null; do\n"
  s = s + "      if [ -r /proc/$pid/status ]; then\n"
  s = s + "        v=$(awk '/VmHWM/{print $2}' /proc/$pid/status 2>/dev/null)\n"
  s = s + "        case $v in\n"
  s = s + "          '') ;;\n"
  s = s + "          *) hwm=$v ;;\n"
  s = s + "        esac\n"
  s = s + "      fi\n"
  // `sed 's/.*) //'` strips through the LAST ')' so a comm field containing
  // its own parens cannot shift the column count; the remaining fields'
  // 12th and 13th are utime+stime — tests/bit/fsblock.bit's `cpu()` reads
  // the same two fields the same way, mutation-tested for #2207.
  s = s + "      if [ -r /proc/$pid/stat ]; then\n"
  s = s + "        c=$(sed 's/.*) //' /proc/$pid/stat 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $12+$13}')\n"
  s = s + "        case $c in\n"
  s = s + "          '') ;;\n"
  s = s + "          *) cpu=$c ;;\n"
  s = s + "        esac\n"
  s = s + "      fi\n"
  s = s + "      sleep 0.05\n"
  s = s + "    done\n"
  s = s + "    wait $pid\n"
  s = s + "    ec=$?\n"
  s = s + "    CLK=$(getconf CLK_TCK 2>/dev/null || echo 100)\n"
  s = s + "    cpuCs=-1\n"
  s = s + "    rssB=-1\n"
  s = s + "    if [ $cpu -ge 0 ]; then cpuCs=$((cpu * 100 / CLK)); fi\n"
  s = s + "    if [ $hwm -ge 0 ]; then rssB=$((hwm * 1024)); fi\n"
  // gcPath is the child's OWN stderr, captured separately above (Linux has no
  // `/usr/bin/time -l` merging it in for us the way the Darwin branch gets
  // for free) — same `swept=`/`live=` scan, different source file.
  s = s +
    "    obj=$(awk 'BEGIN{" +
    gcAwkBody +
    "END{if (sw>=0 && lv>=0) print sw+lv; else print -1}' " +
    shQuote(gcPath) +
    ")\n"
  s = s + "    printf \"%d %d %d %d\\n\" $ec $cpuCs $rssB $obj > " + shQuote(resPath) + "\n"
  s = s + "    exit $ec\n"
  s = s + "    ;;\n"
  s = s + "esac\n"
  return s
}

class Measured {
  ok: bool,
  exitCode: int,
  cpuCentis: int,
  rssBytes: int,
  objects: int,
  out: string,
}

// `trimSpace` (std/strings) only strips spaces and tabs, not newlines — the
// result file's line always ends in the `\n` awk's/printf's own `%s\n`
// emitted, so that has to go before `split` sees it or the last field keeps
// a trailing `\n` and fails `parseInt`.
fn trimTrailingNl(s: string): string {
  let hi = len(s)
  while (hi > 0 && (s[hi - 1] == '\n' || s[hi - 1] == '\r')) {
    hi = hi - 1
  }
  return s[0:hi]
}

fn parseMeasured(resText: string, outText: string): Measured {
  let parts = split(trimSpace(trimTrailingNl(resText)), " ")
  if (len(parts) != 4) {
    return Measured{ ok: false, exitCode: 0, cpuCentis: 0, rssBytes: 0, objects: 0, out: outText }
  }
  let ec = parseInt(parts[0]) catch _ {
    return Measured{ ok: false, exitCode: 0, cpuCentis: 0, rssBytes: 0, objects: 0, out: outText }
  }
  let cpu = parseInt(parts[1]) catch _ {
    return Measured{ ok: false, exitCode: 0, cpuCentis: 0, rssBytes: 0, objects: 0, out: outText }
  }
  let rss = parseInt(parts[2]) catch _ {
    return Measured{ ok: false, exitCode: 0, cpuCentis: 0, rssBytes: 0, objects: 0, out: outText }
  }
  let obj = parseInt(parts[3]) catch _ {
    return Measured{ ok: false, exitCode: 0, cpuCentis: 0, rssBytes: 0, objects: 0, out: outText }
  }
  return Measured{
    ok: true,
    exitCode: ec,
    cpuCentis: cpu,
    rssBytes: rss,
    objects: obj,
    out: outText,
  }
}

// --- tally -----------------------------------------------------------------

class Tally { checks: int, failures: int }

fn markFail(t: Tally, msg: string) {
  t.failures = t.failures + 1
  eprint("FAIL benchgate: " + msg + "\n")
}

fn minInt(a: int, b: int): int {
  if (a < b) {
    return a
  }
  return b
}

// --- one case ----------------------------------------------------------

// One case's build outcome. `binPath` is only meaningful when `ok` — see
// BuildResult{ok: false} sites below, which all leave it "".
class BuildResult { ok: bool, binPath: string }

// Derives this case's paths from `caseScratch`, then writes and runs its
// build script, marking `t` on any failure along the way. Extracted from
// runCase() (#3542) so the build phase carries its own complexity budget
// instead of adding to the driver's; paths are derived here (rather than
// passed in individually) to keep this at the same param count as
// buildScript()/runCase() rather than adding a new E0202 finding.
fn buildBenchCase(
  bit: string, libbitrt: string, stdlib: string, benchDir: string,
  caseScratch: string, name: string, t: Tally,
): BuildResult {
  let srcPath = benchDir + "/" + name + "/" + name + ".bit"
  let binPath = caseScratch + "/bin"
  let buildOut = caseScratch + "/build.out"
  let buildErr = caseScratch + "/build.err"
  let buildSh = caseScratch + "/build.sh"

  writeFile(buildSh, buildScript(bit, libbitrt, stdlib, srcPath, binPath, buildOut, buildErr)) catch e {
    markFail(t, "${name}: cannot write build script: " + e.message())
    return BuildResult{ ok: false, binPath: "" }
  }
  if (fsChmod(buildSh, 448) != 0) { // 0o700
    markFail(t, "${name}: cannot chmod build script")
    return BuildResult{ ok: false, binPath: "" }
  }
  let buildCode = osRunBounded(buildSh, timeoutMs())
  if (buildCode != 0) {
    let berr = readFile(buildErr) catch e {
      ""
    }
    markFail(t, "${name}: build failed (${runMsg(buildCode)}): ${berr}")
    return BuildResult{ ok: false, binPath: "" }
  }
  if (!exists(binPath)) {
    markFail(t, "${name}: build reported success but ${binPath} does not exist")
    return BuildResult{ ok: false, binPath: "" }
  }
  return BuildResult{ ok: true, binPath: binPath }
}

// One rep's launch outcome: the run script written, chmod'd and executed
// through osRunBounded. `launched` is false only for a harness-side failure
// (write/chmod/spawn/timeout/crash — see runMsg()), never for a benchmark
// that ran and produced a bad result; that split is validateRep()'s job.
class RepRun { launched: bool, msg: string, resText: string, outText: string }

// Extracted from runCase() (#3542): the filesystem/subprocess half of one
// repetition, with none of the result parsing.
fn runRepOnce(binPath: string, caseScratch: string, rep: int, gcMinKb: int): RepRun {
  let outPath = caseScratch + "/run${rep}.out"
  let resPath = caseScratch + "/run${rep}.res"
  let runSh = caseScratch + "/run${rep}.sh"
  writeFile(runSh, measureScript(binPath, outPath, resPath, gcMinKb)) catch e {
    return RepRun{
      launched: false,
      msg: "cannot write run script: " + e.message(),
      resText: "",
      outText: "",
    }
  }
  if (fsChmod(runSh, 448) != 0) {
    return RepRun{ launched: false, msg: "cannot chmod run script", resText: "", outText: "" }
  }
  // The wrapper's own exit re-raises the benchmark's exit code (see
  // measureScript's header), so a non-zero, non-timeout, non-signal code
  // here means the BENCHMARK itself failed, not the harness — validateRep()
  // below reads that case from the parsed result line instead.
  let code = osRunBounded(runSh, timeoutMs())
  if (code == 0 - 2 || code == 0 - 1 || code <= 0 - 100) {
    return RepRun{ launched: false, msg: runMsg(code), resText: "", outText: "" }
  }
  let resText = readFile(resPath) catch e {
    ""
  }
  let outText = readFile(outPath) catch e {
    ""
  }
  return RepRun{ launched: true, msg: "", resText: resText, outText: outText }
}

// One rep's measured outcome, parsed and checked against `b`. Never touches
// the filesystem or a subprocess — runRepOnce() above owns that half.
// Extracted from runCase() (#3542).
class RepCheck { ok: bool, msg: string, cpu: int, rss: int, obj: int }

fn validateRep(resText: string, outText: string, b: Baseline): RepCheck {
  let m = parseMeasured(resText, outText)
  if (!m.ok) {
    return RepCheck{
      ok: false,
      msg: "no parseable result line (got '${resText}')",
      cpu: 0,
      rss: 0,
      obj: 0,
    }
  }
  if (m.exitCode != 0) {
    return RepCheck{ ok: false, msg: "benchmark exited ${m.exitCode}", cpu: 0, rss: 0, obj: 0 }
  }
  // objects < 0 covers both a malformed result line AND a missing/absent
  // `BIT_GC_STATS` line (measureScript's awk prints -1 in either case, never
  // a silent 0) — this must be a hard fail unconditionally, even for a case
  // whose Baseline.objects is itself -1 ("not measured on this platform"): a
  // case with no recorded band still needs the underlying instrumentation
  // working, or a future re-baseline would read garbage.
  if (m.cpuCentis < 0 || m.rssBytes < 0 || m.objects < 0) {
    return RepCheck{
      ok: false,
      msg: "measurement unavailable (cpu=${m.cpuCentis} rss=${m.rssBytes} obj=${m.objects}) — " +
        "a -1 here can mean the BIT_GC_STATS line was missing or unparseable; never treat it as 0",
      cpu: 0, rss: 0, obj: 0,
    }
  }
  let got = trimSpace(trimTrailingNl(m.out))
  if (got != b.expected) {
    return RepCheck{
      ok: false,
      msg: "printed '${got}', want '${b.expected}' — wrong answer, cost not trusted",
      cpu: 0, rss: 0, obj: 0,
    }
  }
  return RepCheck{ ok: true, msg: "", cpu: m.cpuCentis, rss: m.rssBytes, obj: m.objects }
}

// Prints the unconditional per-case summary line (#2931) and runs the four
// CPU/RSS/object-count checks against `b`'s ceilings and floor, marking `t`
// for each one that trips. Pure reporting/comparison — no filesystem, no
// subprocess. Extracted from runCase() (#3542).
fn reportAndCheck(b: Baseline, minCpu: int, minRss: int, minObj: int, t: Tally) {
  // CPU reads its own per-case ceiling (#2982); RSS always uses the blanket
  // 1.5x — see cpuCeiling()'s and Baseline.cpuCeilingPct's comments above.
  let cpuCeil = cpuCeiling(b)
  let rssCeil = ceiling(b.rssBytes)
  // objects is TWO-SIDED (#3130) — see objCeiling()/objFloor()'s comment
  // above. Baseline.objects == -1 means "not measured on this platform"; the
  // two checks below are then both skipped, but validateRep() already
  // required the stats line to be present regardless.
  let objCeil = objCeiling(b.objects)
  let objFlr = objFloor(b.objects)
  // Printed unconditionally, pass or fail, so a slow drift toward the
  // ceiling is visible in the suite log before it ever trips (#2931).
  print(
      "benchgate: ${b.name} cpu=${minCpu}cs (baseline ${b.cpuCentis}cs, ceiling ${cpuCeil}cs) " +
      "rss=${minRss}B (baseline ${b.rssBytes}B, ceiling ${rssCeil}B) " +
      "obj=${minObj} (baseline ${b.objects}, floor ${objFlr}, ceiling ${objCeil})\n",
  )

  if (minCpu > cpuCeil) {
    markFail(
      t,
        "${b.name} user CPU regressed: ${minCpu}cs (min of ${reps}), " +
        "baseline ${b.cpuCentis}cs, ceiling ${cpuCeil}cs (${b.cpuCeilingPct}%)",
    )
  }
  if (minRss > rssCeil) {
    markFail(
      t,
        "${b.name} peak RSS regressed: ${minRss}B (min of ${reps}), " +
        "baseline ${b.rssBytes}B, ceiling ${rssCeil}B (150%)",
    )
  }
  if (b.objects >= 0 && minObj > objCeil) {
    markFail(
      t,
        "${b.name} object count regressed: ${minObj} (min of ${reps}), " +
        "baseline ${b.objects}, ceiling ${objCeil} (${objCeilPct}%)",
    )
  }
  if (b.objects >= 0 && minObj < objFlr) {
    markFail(
      t,
        "${b.name} object count fell below floor — an unrecorded improvement: " +
        "${minObj} (min of ${reps}), baseline ${b.objects}, floor ${objFlr} " +
        "(${objFloorPct}%) — re-measure and update Baseline.objects in " +
        "tests/bit/benchgate/baselines.bit if this is real",
    )
  }
}

// Builds the case once, runs it `reps` times, and checks the minimum against
// `b`'s baselines. Split for complexity (#3542) into buildBenchCase() (the
// build), runRepOnce()+validateRep() (one repetition's launch and its
// result), and reportAndCheck() (the ceiling/floor comparisons) — this
// function is now only the sequencing between them.
fn runCase(
  bit: string, libbitrt: string, stdlib: string, benchDir: string,
  scratch: string, b: Baseline, t: Tally,
) {
  let caseScratch = scratch + "/" + b.name
  mkdirQuiet(caseScratch)

  let built = buildBenchCase(bit, libbitrt, stdlib, benchDir, caseScratch, b.name, t)
  if (!built.ok) {
    return
  }
  let binPath = built.binPath

  // Sentinels above any measurement this gate could ever legitimately see —
  // the check after the loop proves a real run replaced them, the same shape
  // tests/bit/schedbench.bit's `benchTrials` uses.
  let minCpu = 1000000000
  let minRss = 1000000000
  let minObj = 1000000000
  let sawOne = false

  let rep = 0
  while (rep < reps) {
    let rr = runRepOnce(binPath, caseScratch, rep, b.gcMinKb)
    if (!rr.launched) {
      markFail(t, "${b.name} rep ${rep}: ${rr.msg}")
      rep = rep + 1
      continue
    }
    let rc = validateRep(rr.resText, rr.outText, b)
    if (!rc.ok) {
      markFail(t, "${b.name} rep ${rep}: ${rc.msg}")
      rep = rep + 1
      continue
    }
    minCpu = minInt(minCpu, rc.cpu)
    minRss = minInt(minRss, rc.rss)
    minObj = minInt(minObj, rc.obj)
    sawOne = true
    rep = rep + 1
  }

  if (!sawOne) {
    markFail(t, "${b.name}: no successful run out of ${reps}")
    return
  }

  reportAndCheck(b, minCpu, minRss, minObj, t)
  t.checks = t.checks + 1
}

// --- driver ------------------------------------------------------------

// Fails the whole gate immediately (never returns) when `value` — expected
// to be an existing path read from `label`'s env var — is empty or does not
// exist. Extracted from main() (#3542): the three BIT_BIN/BIT_LIBBITRT/
// BIT_BENCH_DIR checks each cost main() its own branch inline.
fn requireEnvPath(label: string, value: string) {
  if (value == "" || !exists(value)) {
    eprint("FAIL benchgate: ${label}='${value}' does not exist — refusing to guess\n")
    exit(1)
  }
}

fn main() {
  let bit = envOr("BIT_BIN", "")
  requireEnvPath("BIT_BIN", bit)
  let libbitrt = envOr("BIT_LIBBITRT", "")
  requireEnvPath("BIT_LIBBITRT", libbitrt)
  let benchDir = envOr("BIT_BENCH_DIR", "")
  requireEnvPath("BIT_BENCH_DIR", benchDir)
  let stdlib = env("BIT_STDLIB")

  let target = hostTarget()
  let table = baselinesFor(target)
  if (len(table) == 0) {
    eprint(
        "FAIL benchgate: no baseline recorded for ${platformName(target)}; " +
        "measure one on a quiet host and add it to tests/bit/benchgate/baselines.bit\n",
    )
    exit(1)
  }

  let root = scratchRoot()
  mkdirQuiet(root)

  let t = Tally{ checks: 0, failures: 0 }
  for b of table {
    runCase(bit, libbitrt, stdlib, benchDir, root, b, t)
  }

  // Compared against the actual table length, not a hardcoded count (#2978):
  // platforms register different numbers of cases (baselinesLinuxX64 lacks
  // "churn"), so a fixed constant here would under-count a genuine failure
  // on whichever platform has the larger table.
  if (t.checks < len(table)) {
    eprint("FAIL benchgate: only ${t.checks} of ${len(table)} cases completed\n")
    t.failures = t.failures + 1
  }
  if (t.failures > 0) {
    println("benchgate: FAIL — ${t.failures} problem(s) across ${t.checks} case(s)")
    exit(1)
  }
  println("benchgate: PASS — ${t.checks} case(s) within their RSS/CPU/object baselines")
}
